From 71a84426d4800da9d567b8d8279b79f44ef4d33b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 04:40:24 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] escha vulkan: f16-native generator - decode +18%, bit-exact

The profile after the RTE fix says ESCHA_MM is still 86.95% of decode GPU time (13,200 calls at
642 us), so the bottleneck did not move and the generator is still the whole cost.

unpackHalf2x16 widens both halves to f32, adds there, narrows back to f16 and widens again for the
FMA. unpackFloat2x16 reinterprets the same bits as float16_t with no convert at all, so the add is
an f16 add and only one widening survives - roughly three instructions of eight removed from the
hottest line in the model.

Bit-identical, and proven so rather than argued: an f16 add is the exact sum rounded once, and the
sum of two f16 values is always exact in f32, so both spellings round the same real number the same
way. The exhaustive 65,536-codeword sweep carries a fourth column for this path - CANDIDATE EXACT,
all 524,288 words matching the shipped path bit for bit.

Measured, two rounds, arms alternated in one thermal session on Balanced:
    f32 generator   pp512 15.46 / 15.44   tg32 3.27 / 3.28
    f16 generator   pp512 16.20 / 16.43   tg32 3.87 / 3.86
That is +18.0% decode and +5.6% prefill for zero quality cost.

ESCHA_F16_GEN=0 restores the f32 path. The one device behaviour that could separate them is f16
denormal flushing; this device reports denorm-preserve TRUE. Re-sweep on any device change.

Origin: ArifiLabs (native)
---
 .../ggml-vulkan/vulkan-shaders/escha_mm.comp  | 28 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+)

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/vulkan-shaders/escha_mm.comp b/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/vulkan-shaders/escha_mm.comp
index 8af9a8f57..d8c97d1c1 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/vulkan-shaders/escha_mm.comp
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/vulkan-shaders/escha_mm.comp
@@ -113,8 +113,35 @@ uint f32_to_f16_rte(float f) {
 #define ESCHA_HW_RTE 1
 #endif
 
+// SEAT-40 LEVER: stay in f16 for the whole generator.
+//
+// The profile says ESCHA_MM is 86.95% of decode GPU time (13,200 calls, 642 us each), so the
+// generator is still the whole cost after the RTE fix -- the bottleneck did not move.
+//
+// unpackHalf2x16 widens both halves to f32 (two V_CVT_F32_F16), adds in f32, then narrows back
+// (one V_CVT_F16_F32) and widens again for the FMA. unpackFloat2x16 reinterprets the same bits as
+// float16_t with no convert at all, so the add happens in f16 and only ONE widening survives.
+// Three instructions of roughly eight, on the single hottest line in the model.
+//
+// It is bit-identical, not approximately so: an f16 add is the exact sum rounded once to f16, and
+// the sum of two f16 values is always exact in f32, so both spellings round the same real number
+// the same way. PROVEN, not argued -- the exhaustive 65,536-codeword sweep carries a fourth column
+// comparing this against the shipped path: "CANDIDATE EXACT: F16ADD matches the SHIPPED F16CAST
+// path on all 524288 words, bit for bit" (rte-sweep/RTE-SWEEP-RESULT.txt).
+//
+// The device reports denorm-preserve TRUE for f16, which is the one behaviour that could have
+// separated the two paths. On a device that flushes f16 denormals this must be re-swept before it
+// is trusted; ESCHA_F16_GEN=0 restores the f32 path.
+#ifndef ESCHA_F16_GEN
+#define ESCHA_F16_GEN 1
+#endif
+
 float escha_F(uint xw) {
     const uint q = 0x3B603B60u ^ ((xw * 0xCBAC1FEDu) & 0x8FFF8FFFu);
+#if ESCHA_F16_GEN
+    const f16vec2 hh = unpackFloat2x16(q);         // pure bit reinterpret, no convert
+    return float(hh.x + hh.y);                     // one f16 add, one widen
+#else
     const vec2 h = unpackHalf2x16(q);
     const float s = h.x + h.y;                     // exact operands; correctly-rounded f32 add
 #if ESCHA_HW_RTE
@@ -122,6 +149,7 @@ float escha_F(uint xw) {
 #else
     return unpackHalf2x16(f32_to_f16_rte(s)).x;    // value of the f16-rounded sum
 #endif
+#endif
 }
 
 // 64-bit funnel shift via two u32s; no shaderInt64
